---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Spades Rules: How to Play (Complete Guide) | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Learn the rules of Spades: partnerships, bidding, nil, why spades are always trump, breaking spades, bags, and how to score to 500.';
const updated = '2026-06-14';
const heading = 'Spades Rules: How to Play';
const subtitle =
  'Everything you need to play Spades correctly — the partnership, the bid, why spades always trump, how tricks and bags are scored, and the all-or-nothing nil bid.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Spades Rules', href: '/guides/spades-rules/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Are spades always trump in Spades?',
    a: 'Yes. The spade suit is permanent trump every hand. A spade beats any card of the led suit, and the highest spade played wins a trick. You may only lead a spade once spades have been "broken".',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a nil bid?',
    a: 'A nil bid is a promise to take zero tricks that hand. Make it and your team gains 100 points; take even one trick and you lose 100. Your partner still plays their own bid, so a successful nil is a huge swing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are bags in Spades?',
    a: 'Bags (or overtricks) are tricks your team wins beyond its combined bid. Each bag is worth 1 point, but they are a trap: every time your accumulated bags reach 10, you are penalised 100 points. Overbidding to dump tricks is risky.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Spades Rules: How to Play (Complete Guide)',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jared', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/spades-rules/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/spades.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/spades/', label: 'Play Spades' }}
>
  <p>
    Spades is a partnership trick-taking game built around one promise: before a card is played, you
    predict how many tricks you'll win, and then you have to deliver. Spades are always trump, the
    Queen of Spades has no special meaning here, and the first team to <strong>500</strong> wins.
    This page is a complete reference to the rules. To learn by doing, you can
    <a href="/spades/">play Spades</a> now with a computer partner against two AI opponents.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Spades?</h2>
  <p>
    Spades is a four-player game played in two <strong>partnerships</strong> — you and the player
    across the table versus the other two. It uses a standard 52-card deck, aces are high, and the
    spade suit is <strong>permanent trump</strong>. Each hand has 13 tricks, and the heart of the
    game is bidding: your team commits to a number of tricks and is scored on whether it hits that
    number.
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    All 52 cards are dealt out evenly, <strong>13 to each player</strong>. There is no stock and
    nothing left over. You look at your hand and assess how many tricks it can realistically take.
  </p>

  <h2>Bidding</h2>
  <p>
    Going around the table, each player announces a <strong>bid</strong> — the number of tricks
    (0 to 13) they expect to take. Your team's bid is the <strong>sum</strong> of both partners'
    bids; that combined number is your contract for the hand. A bid of <strong>nil</strong> (zero)
    is a special promise to take no tricks at all, scored separately and richly rewarded. You bid
    based only on your own hand, so reading your high spades and side-suit aces accurately is the
    whole skill.
  </p>

  <h2>Spades are trump</h2>
  <p>
    The spade suit beats every other suit. If you cannot follow the led suit, you may play a spade to
    <strong>trump</strong> the trick and win it. When two or more spades are played to a trick, the
    <strong>highest spade</strong> wins. If no spade is played, the highest card of the led suit wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Following suit and breaking spades</h2>
  <p>
    You <strong>must follow the led suit</strong> if you hold a card in it. Only when you are void in
    the led suit may you trump with a spade or discard. You may <strong>not lead a spade</strong>
    until spades have been "broken" — that happens the first time a player, unable to follow suit,
    plays a spade. After that, spades can be led freely. (You may always lead a spade if your hand
    contains nothing but spades.)
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring</h2>
  <p>At the end of each hand, score each team:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Made your bid:</strong> score <strong>10 points per bid trick</strong>. A team that bid
      7 and took at least 7 scores 70.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Overtricks (bags):</strong> each trick beyond your bid is a <strong>bag</strong> worth
      <strong>1 point</strong> — but they pile up dangerously (see below).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Missed your bid:</strong> if your team takes fewer tricks than it bid, you are "set" and
      <strong>lose 10 points per bid trick</strong> — bidding 7 and taking 6 costs you 70.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The bag penalty</h2>
  <p>
    Bags are tempting because each is a point, but they accumulate across hands. Every time your
    running bag count reaches <strong>10</strong>, your team is hit with a <strong>100-point
    penalty</strong> and the counter rolls over. That single rule is why you bid honestly: collecting
    overtricks feels good until ten of them erase a whole game's progress.
  </p>

  <h2>Nil bids</h2>
  <p>
    Bidding <strong>nil</strong> means you promise to win <strong>zero</strong> tricks. Succeed and
    your team gains <strong>100 points</strong>; take even one trick and you lose 100. Your partner
    still plays their own bid normally and will often try to "cover" you — winning tricks you can't
    safely duck. A nil is the boldest play in Spades and can decide a game on its own.
  </p>

  <h2>How you win</h2>
  <p>
    Hands continue until a team reaches the target of <strong>500 points</strong>. The team with the
    higher score at that point wins. Because a set or a blown nil can cost 70–100 points in a single
    hand, the lead changes hands often and accurate bidding wins games.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, <a href="/spades/">play a hand of Spades</a> with a computer partner.
    To bid sharper and set your opponents, read our
    <a href="/guides/spades-strategy/">Spades strategy guide</a>, see how it compares in
    <a href="/guides/hearts-vs-spades/">Hearts vs Spades</a>, try the avoidance game in
    <a href="/guides/hearts-rules/">Hearts</a>, or browse the <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
